  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1128 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nThrough Friday\n\nA surface high pressure will provide dry weather through \nFriday. The HRRR is indicating that the northwest winds aloft \nwill bring elevated smoke into the area on Friday afternoon. The\nhighest concentrations will be along and north of Interstate \n90. While elevated smoke does move into the area, southerly \nwinds will keep surface smoke from moving into the Driftless \nArea. This surface smoke gets as far east as the central Dakotas\nand then southerly winds abruptly stops its eastward progress.\n\nUnlike the past 2 mornings, high clouds, dry dew points, and \nincreasing winds in the boundary layer will likely limit \ndevelopment of fog in the Wisconsin River Valley and the \nMississippi and Wisconsin River tributaries.\n\nLows will be in the 50s tonight. High temperatures on Friday\nwill range from the mid-70s to lower 80s.\n\nFriday night and Saturday\n\nA shortwave trough will move east through the Upper Mississippi\nRiver Valley overnight Friday and Saturday morning. In response\nof this system, a Canadian cold front will move southeast into\nthe region. This front will get as far south as either the\nInterstate 90 or 94 corridor. Scattered showers and a few storms\nwill accompany this front. With lessening instability during the\nnight, kept precipitation chances generally less than 30 percent\nmainly north of Interstate 94. Low temperatures will range from\nthe upper 50s to mid-60s.\n\nDuring the afternoon, the front will move back to the north as a\nwarm front as the 500 mb ridge build across the region. High\ntemperatures will range from the mid-80s to lower 90s. Heat\nindices will be mainly in the 90s. Closer to Interstate 35, the\nheat indices may be around 100.\n\nSaturday night into Sunday\n\nIt still looks like a mesoscale convective complex will develop\nupstream of the Upper Mississippi River Valley and then move\nsoutheast through the region during the overnight. There\ncontinues to be much uncertainty on the track of this system.\nWith the 0-3 km and 0-6 km shear and instability looking\nstronger, there might be a threat of severe weather. With\nenhanced winds aloft, the primary threat looks to be damaging\nwinds. In addition, large hail and isolated tornadoes could be\nlesser threats.\n\nLow temperatures will range from the mid-60s to lower 70s on\nSaturday night. High temperatures will range from the mid-80s to\nmid-90s. The heat indices look to be a bit higher (ranging from\naround 100 to 105) along and west of the Mississippi River. It\ncontinues to look like a Heat Advisory will be needed.\n\nSunday Night into Monday Night\n\nThe 500 mb ridge will gradually retrograde west and southwest\nearly next week. As this occurs, a Canadian cold front will move\nsouth through the region. Severe weather chances will be highly\ndependent upon when this front moves through the region. If it\ncan move through the afternoon and evening, our probabilities \nwould be higher due to the higher instability. If it moves \nthrough the area later in the evening and overnight, the \nprobabilities would dramatically fall. \n\nLow temperatures on Sunday night would range from the upper 60s\nto mid-70s. High temperatures on Monday have increased and they\nnow range from the mid-80s to mid-90s. Heat indices will be in\nthe 90s north of Interstate 90 and range from around 100 to 105\nsouth of Interstate 90. Another Heat Advisory may be needed for\nnortheast Iowa and southwest Wisconsin.",
